diff --git a/docs/release-notes.md b/docs/release-notes.md
index e44e3a8..eb60698 100644
--- a/docs/release-notes.md
+++ b/docs/release-notes.md
@@ -5,6 +5,20 @@
 ### Bug Fixes
 
 - Fixed concurrent probe shutdowns panicking with `close of closed channel`. Probe starts and stops are now serialized, and a pending initial-delay start cannot revive a stopped or superseded probe.
+- Fixed a restarted process failing with `address already in use` (or otherwise colliding with itself) because the new instance was launched while the old one was still shutting down. This hit [file-watch](https://f1bonacc1.github.io/process-compose/watch/) restarts in particular, which skip the restart backoff that used to hide it. A restart now waits until the old instance - its [whole process group](https://f1bonacc1.github.io/process-compose/launcher/#when-is-a-process-stopped), not just a shell wrapper in front of the real server - has exited. For the same reason a stopped process now stays `Terminating` until its process group is gone, and with `shutdown.timeout_seconds` set, group members that outlive the command are killed at the timeout as documented. Without a timeout a process that ignores its stop signal is never killed, so a restart now waits for it instead of starting a second copy next to it.
+- Fixed a process that had exited still showing as `Running`, and its `process_completed` / `process_completed_successfully` dependents never starting, when something it started in the background kept its `stdout` or `stderr` open (a migration script launching a helper, `pg_ctl start` without a log file). A process now completes when its command exits. The output of whatever it left behind is still captured.
+- Fixed `process-compose` exiting with `255` instead of the exit code of the process that ended the project. With more than one process carrying `exit_on_end` or `exit_on_failure`, the ones terminated by the shutdown overwrote the exit code of the process that caused it - so a failing test run reported `255` rather than its own code, and so did a passing one. The first process to end the project now decides its exit code, and a shutdown requested with `Ctrl+C` or `process-compose down` exits with `0`.
+- Fixed `process-compose run` terminating itself and the script that called it (`Terminated`, exit `143`) when the project shut down while the foreground process was still running, for instance because a dependency with `exit_on_failure` had failed. The caller never received the exit code and never reached its own clean-up. The foreground process shares the process group of `process-compose`, and stopping it signalled that whole group; it and its child processes are now signalled individually. The same applied to stopping an elevated process without the TUI.
+- Fixed `process-compose` leaving all of its processes running, ports included, when it was ended by `SIGQUIT` (`Ctrl+\`) - which also printed a dump of every goroutine - or by its output being closed (`process-compose up -t=false | head`). Both now [shut the processes down first](https://f1bonacc1.github.io/process-compose/launcher/#stopping-process-compose-itself), like `Ctrl+C` does. For a goroutine dump, send `SIGABRT`.
+- A second signal during a shutdown now forces it: the remaining processes are killed with `SIGKILL` and `process-compose` exits with `128 + signal`. Previously further signals were ignored, so a shutdown held up by a process that ignores its stop signal could only be escaped by killing `process-compose` itself, leaving that process and any others behind. Signals delivered together with the first one do not count.
+- Fixed stopping or restarting a process with [`is_tty`](https://f1bonacc1.github.io/process-compose/configuration/#pseudo-terminals) cutting off its log at the stop signal and corrupting its exit code. Its terminal was closed the moment the signal was sent, so everything it wrote while shutting down was lost and failed to write - which is why a program that exited cleanly could be reported with a code such as `120`, the status some runtimes use for a failed flush at exit. It was also sent `SIGHUP` in place of the configured `SIGTERM`. Such a process is now stopped like any other: it gets the configured signal, its shutdown output is logged, and it reports its own exit code. A tty process that relied on being hung up - one that ignores `SIGTERM` - needs `shutdown.signal: 1` or `shutdown.timeout_seconds`. Interactive processes (`is_interactive`) are still ended by hanging up.
+- Fixed processes terminated by a signal reporting exit code `-1`. They now report `128 + signal` (`143` for `SIGTERM`, `137` for `SIGKILL`), as [documented](https://f1bonacc1.github.io/process-compose/launcher/#successful-exit-codes), which also makes such exits matchable by `success_exit_codes`. Anything that compares a process `exit_code` to `-1` needs updating.
+- Fixed a process being killed shortly after it was restarted by a failed readiness probe when `shutdown.timeout_seconds` was set: the `SIGKILL` armed for the instance being stopped was delivered to its replacement once the timeout expired.
+- Fixed a process that was stopped, or restarted, at the very moment it was being launched starting anyway, and one that ended at the very moment it was stopped being left in `Terminating`.
+- Fixed a process launched while the project was shutting down - by an in-flight restart or a schedule - being left running after `process-compose` had exited.
+- Stopping an [`is_daemon`](https://f1bonacc1.github.io/process-compose/launcher/#background-detached-processes) process that has no `shutdown.command` no longer leaves it stuck in `Terminating`, which also made the next project shutdown hang. A daemon that has detached from the process group of its launcher cannot be reached by a signal: stopping or restarting it now fails with an error that says so and leaves it `Launched`, since it is still running, and a project shutdown leaves it running with a warning instead of hanging. A daemon that is still in that group is signalled and reported as stopped once it has really exited.
+- Fixed a possible `send on closed channel` crash when a process wrote to its `log_location` file at the moment that log was being closed.
+- The TUI no longer freezes while a process is being restarted with the restart shortcut.
 
